#ddev-generated
# Settings for `ddev worktree` (ddev-craft-worktree add-on).
#
# On install this template is copied to .ddev/worktree.conf only if that file doesn't
# exist yet; edit and commit worktree.conf, never this .example (updates replace it).
# It is sourced by bash, so use bash array syntax. Every path is relative to the project
# root. Uncomment and edit what you need; anything left commented keeps the default shown.
#
# Front-end builds and other setup belong in DDEV's own `hooks: post-start:` in
# .ddev/config.yaml, and upload_dirs there too: worktrees inherit both from git.
#
# WORKTREE_POST_START and WORKTREE_POST_IMPORT commands can use $WORKTREE_URL,
# $WORKTREE_PROJECT, $WORKTREE_DIR, $MAIN_URL, $MAIN_PROJECT and $MAIN_ROOT.

# Untracked files copied from the main checkout. Copied .env* files have their
# ://<project>.ddev.site URLs repointed at the worktree's own hostname.
# Default: (.env), plus config/license.key for craftcms projects.
# WORKTREE_COPY_FILES=(.env config/license.key)

# Untracked directories cloned from the main checkout, such as local asset volumes. On
# macOS (APFS) and reflink-capable Linux filesystems this is copy-on-write, so a worktree
# uses no extra disk for them until a file changes. Default: none.
# WORKTREE_CLONE_DIRS=(web/assets)

# Commands run inside the new worktree after `ddev start` (and its DDEV hooks).
# Default: ("ddev composer install") if there is a composer.json, otherwise none.
# WORKTREE_POST_START=("ddev composer install")

# Commands run after the database has been imported.
# Default: ("ddev craft up --interactive=0") for craftcms projects, otherwise none.
# WORKTREE_POST_IMPORT=("ddev craft up --interactive=0")
# WordPress keeps its URL in the database:
# WORKTREE_POST_IMPORT=('ddev wp search-replace "$MAIN_URL" "$WORKTREE_URL" --all-tables')

# Keep seed dumps here (for --reuse-db) instead of deleting them after import.
# Never point this at .ddev/db_snapshots. An environment variable of the same name wins.
# WORKTREE_DB_DIR="$HOME/ddev-worktree-dbs"
